 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Training Time:** Aggressive truncation (e.g., $1\sigma$) significantly reduces training time (2m26s vs 4m31s for default) [S1, unofficial]. However, applying the modification itself adds slight overhead compared to the original truncated logic at the same sigma level (e.g., $3.33\sigma$ with modification takes 5m07s vs 4m31s default) [S1, unofficial].
-- **Memory Usage:** VRAM usage decreases with more aggressive truncation (4.5GiB at $1\sigma$ vs 6.1GiB default) [S1, unofficial].
-- **Gaussian Count:** Fewer Gaussians are created during densification when truncating aggressively (1.39M at $1\sigma$ vs 2.70M default) [S1, unofficial].
-- **Quality (PSNR):** Aggressive truncation reduces quality. $1\sigma$ yields 25.86 PSNR, $2\sigma$ yields 27.23 PSNR, while default $3.33\sigma$ yields 27.54 PSNR on Mip-NeRF360 scenes [S1, unofficial].
-- **Strengths:** Enables consistent truncation at low standard deviations; allows gradients to flow to low-opacity Gaussians; reduces memory and training time for high-opacity scenes.
-- **Limitations:** Reduced PSNR with aggressive truncation; slower training than original 3DGS at equivalent sigma levels due to the extra evaluation step; may produce artifacts if truncation is too aggressive for transparent regions.
+**Time and Memory Complexity:**
+- Truncation at 1σ with modification: 2m26s training time, 4.5GiB VRAM, 1.39M Gaussians [S1, unofficial].
+- Truncation at 2σ with modification: 3m41s training time, 5.5GiB VRAM, 2.22M Gaussians [S1, unofficial].
+- Default 3.33σ: 4m31s training time, 6.1GiB VRAM, 2.70M Gaussians [S1, unofficial].
+- The modification slows down training compared to the default implementation due to the additional checks and gradient computations [S1, unofficial].
+
+**Strengths and Limitations:**
+- **Strengths:** Allows for more aggressive truncation (fewer standard deviations) without losing visibility of low-opacity Gaussians. Enables gradient flow for near-zero opacity Gaussians, which can improve optimization flexibility [S1, unofficial].
+- **Limitations:** Aggressive truncation (1σ) reduces quality (PSNR drops to 25.86) and forces Gaussians to be close to opaque (min opacity ≈ 0.61) [S1, unofficial]. Training time increases with the modification [S1, unofficial].
+
+**When to Use:**
+- Use when investigating gradient behavior for low-opacity Gaussians or when seeking to reduce Gaussian count significantly at the cost of some quality [S1, unofficial].
+- Not recommended for final production renders where maximum PSNR is required, as default truncation (3.33σ) yields higher PSNR (27.54) [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-This method was proposed in "Faster-GS: Analyzing and Improving Gaussian Splatting Optimization" [S1, unofficial]. It addresses limitations in the original 3DGS implementation where the opacity reset clip value of $1/100$ enforced an upper bound on the effective truncation threshold, preventing consistent truncation at fewer than $3.03\sigma$ [S1, unofficial]. The modification also responds to observations by Hahlbohm et al. regarding opacity gradient computation [S1, unofficial].
+Originated in the analysis of 3D Gaussian Splatting optimization, specifically in the paper "Faster-GS: Analyzing and Improving Gaussian Splatting Optimization" [S1, unofficial]. The method addresses limitations in the original 3DGS implementation where opacity was coupled with truncation, preventing consistent truncation at fewer than 3.03 standard deviations [S1, unofficial]. It was proposed to allow for opacity-independent truncation and to address issues with opacity gradient computation [S1, unofficial].
 
